{
  "$schema": "./node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "portfolio",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-28",
  "build": {
    "command": "npm run build"
  },
  // The site is a fully static Astro build. The Worker exists only so the
  // Contact form has a real submission path: Cloudflare serves any request
  // that matches a built asset before the Worker runs, so effectively only
  // POST /api/brief reaches it. Anything else it does receive is passed to
  // the ASSETS binding below, so the site keeps serving either way.
  "main": "./worker/index.ts",
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./dist",
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    "not_found_handling": "404-page",
    // run_worker_first is load-bearing, not a precaution. Without it the asset
    // router claims /api/brief before the Worker runs: a request carrying
    // Accept: text/html gets matched by not_found_handling and answered 405,
    // so any browser-navigation POST to the endpoint fails while a fetch()
    // POST still works. Routing the API prefix to the Worker first fixes it.
    "run_worker_first": ["/api/*"]
  },
  "vars": {
    // Where briefs are delivered. This is the public contact address and is
    // deliberately unchanged by the domain migration — it is a Gmail mailbox,
    // not a website URL, and it is the same address shown on /contact/, in the
    // no-JavaScript fallback and in every mailto link.
    "BRIEF_TO": "soufianeaberbach@gmail.com",
    // Transactional sender identity. NOT a mailbox anyone writes to, and not
    // the public address: Resend requires the From domain to be one it has
    // verified, and a gmail.com sender cannot be verified by a third-party
    // relay. NOT YET VERIFIED — aberbach.co has to be added as a sending
    // domain in Resend and the records Resend itself generates added to DNS
    // before any mail will leave. See docs/DOMAIN-MIGRATION.md.
    "BRIEF_FROM": "brief@aberbach.co",
    // Hostnames a Turnstile solve may legitimately come from, comma-separated.
    // NOT a secret — it is an allowlist, not a credential. Checked against the
    // hostname siteverify reports, so a token minted on a copy of this page
    // hosted somewhere else is refused. Required once TURNSTILE_SECRET is set:
    // an empty or missing value rejects every submission rather than quietly
    // skipping the check. Loopback names are never accepted, whatever is
    // listed here. Add any additional production hostname that serves the
    // Contact page to this list, or its visitors cannot submit.
    //
    // www is listed alongside the apex on purpose, as migration insurance.
    // The canonical host is the apex and www 301s to it, so in normal
    // operation a widget is only ever solved on aberbach.co and www never
    // appears here. But if the www redirect is mis-set or briefly missing, a
    // visitor could reach the Contact page on www — and with www absent from
    // this list the Worker would fail closed and refuse their brief. Keeping
    // both costs nothing: neither host is a third party, and the allowlist
    // still refuses every other hostname on earth. Narrow to the apex alone
    // once the redirect has been verified stable in production.
    "TURNSTILE_HOSTNAMES": "aberbach.co,www.aberbach.co"
  }
  // ── ACTIVATION ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // The form declines briefs until 1-3 below exist. Verified against the
  // wrangler in devDependencies (4.x); `npx wrangler kv namespace --help` is
  // the authority if the CLI moves on.
  //
  // 1. DURABLE STORAGE — required. Until BRIEFS is bound, POST /api/brief
  //    answers 503 and the page tells the visitor to email directly. Nothing
  //    is ever accepted and then dropped.
  //
  //      npx wrangler kv namespace list          # check before creating
  //      npx wrangler kv namespace create portfolio-BRIEFS \
  //        --binding BRIEFS --update-config
  //
  //    --update-config writes the kv_namespaces entry into this file with the
  //    real id, so the id is never typed by hand. Doing it manually instead,
  //    the entry is a sibling of "vars" above:
  //
  //      "kv_namespaces": [{ "binding": "BRIEFS", "id": "<real id>" }]
  //
  //    NO PLACEHOLDER. An invented or preview id here deploys a Worker that
  //    writes into a namespace nobody reads, which is worse than the 503.
  //
  // 2. SECRETS — never committed, never in this file. See .dev.vars.example
  //    for the local equivalents.
  //
  //      npx wrangler secret put RESEND_API_KEY
  //      npx wrangler secret put TURNSTILE_SECRET
  //
  //    BRIEF_FROM above must be on a domain verified in Resend, or Resend
  //    rejects the send. The brief still survives: it is already in KV and an
  //    undelivered:<reference> record is written alongside it.
  //
  //    aberbach.co IS NOT VERIFIED YET. Add it as a sending domain in Resend,
  //    then add the DNS records Resend generates for it — they are per-domain
  //    values and must be copied from the dashboard, never guessed — and wait
  //    for Resend to report the domain verified. BRIEF_TO is a Gmail mailbox
  //    and needs no verification; only the sender does.
  //
  // 3. TURNSTILE — ACTIVATE BOTH HALVES TOGETHER OR NEITHER.
  //
  //    TURNSTILE_SECRET is what the Worker verifies against. When it is
  //    ABSENT, Turnstile verification is disabled entirely and submissions are
  //    screened by the honeypot, the submit-timing check, the per-IP rate
  //    limit, full field validation and — for native form posts — an origin
  //    check. That is the safe pre-activation state.
  //
  //    When TURNSTILE_SECRET IS CONFIGURED, EVERY submission to /api/brief
  //    requires a valid Turnstile token, whatever its content type. Content
  //    type is not a credential, so there is no form-urlencoded or "no-JS"
  //    exemption. Verification FAILS CLOSED: a missing or invalid token, a
  //    non-ok siteverify response, or a thrown fetch all reject the
  //    submission. Visitors without JavaScript cannot produce a token and so
  //    do not use the form at all — BriefForm hides it and shows a direct
  //    email fallback instead.
  //
  //    success:true is not the whole check. A token only proves that some
  //    widget on this account was solved somewhere, so the Worker also
  //    requires the solve's CONTEXT to match:
  //
  //      action    must be "contact_brief", which is data-action on the widget
  //                in BriefForm. Both sides must be changed together.
  //      hostname  must appear in TURNSTILE_HOSTNAMES above.
  //
  //    So a widget created in the Cloudflare dashboard must be configured for
  //    the same hostnames listed in that variable. A solve from a different
  //    widget, or from a copy of the page hosted elsewhere, is refused.
  //
  //    PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITEKEY is read by Astro AT BUILD TIME and is what
  //    renders the widget. `wrangler deploy` runs the build command above
  //    itself, so the variable must be present in the environment of THAT
  //    deploy — not merely exported for some earlier, separate build. Set it
  //    in the Cloudflare project's build environment variables, or inline on
  //    the deploy itself:
  //
  //      PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITEKEY=0x... npx wrangler deploy
  //
  //    It is the SITE key, which is public and appears in the page source.
  //    The secret key never goes in this variable.
  //
  //    If TURNSTILE_SECRET is set but PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITEKEY was missing
  //    from the deploy build, the Worker demands a token the page cannot
  //    produce and the form rejects every submission. If the site key is set
  //    but the secret is not, the widget renders and is simply not verified.
  //    The first failure mode is the dangerous one — activate them together.
  //
  // 4. NOT PRODUCTION READY until 1-3 above are done and one real brief has
  //    been received end to end. A successful build proves none of that.
  //    Submitted briefs are kept for 90 days (RETENTION_DAYS in
  //    worker/index.ts), which /privacy/ states verbatim — change both
  //    together. Rate-limit keys hold a truncated SHA-256 of the caller's IP,
  //    never the address itself, and expire after an hour.
  //
  //    docs/BACKEND-ACTIVATION.md is the step-by-step runbook, including how
  //    to verify each piece and what to check after the first live brief.
}
